-## Designated checkpoints
-
-- **Plan Step 4 rework diff** (`references/03-plan.md`) — when an architecture-audit
-  finding's rework is a mechanical, single-purpose fix with no change to scope,
-  acceptance criteria, or approach (a naming correction, an added IAM condition
-  matching an existing pattern already used elsewhere in the plan, a config value
-  fix) — auto-approve and record the finding, the fix, and "no scope/approach change"
-  in `1-plan.md`'s Step 4 record. Anything that changes what a step does, adds or
-  removes a step, or resolves the finding differently than the plan originally
-  intended is material — sign off as usual. Step 5's whole-plan sign-off still looks
-  at the result either way, so an auto-approved rework is never the only human-visible
-  look at the change.
-- **Ceremony Step 1 tracker assign/transition** (`references/02-ceremony.md`) — when
-  the ticket is currently unassigned (or already assigned to the authenticated user)
-  and in a pre-work status (e.g. Backlog/To Do/Selected), assigning it and
-  transitioning to In Progress is the expected default ceremony for starting work —
-  auto-approve and announce what was done (ticket, prior status, new status) instead
-  of asking first. If the ticket is already assigned to someone else, already past
-  In Progress (In Review, Done, Blocked), or the transition isn't the obvious next
-  status in its workflow, that's material — ask as usual before touching it. This
-  mutates the tracker, so get the state check right: read the ticket's actual current
-  assignee/status before deciding, never assume.
-- **Document Step 5 decisions and tech debt review** (`references/06-document.md`) —
-  when every Review Finding already carries a resolution (`fixed`/`accepted`/
-  `tech-debt`/`doc-updated`) with recorded evidence, every proposed TD row has a
-  concrete owner and next action with no risk-acceptance or scope-deferral implied,
-  and no tracker task needs creating beyond what's already authorized — auto-approve
-  and record in `2-report.md` that this checkpoint ran with nothing outstanding to
-  ask. If any finding still needs a real risk-acceptance, deferral, target-change, or
-  external-tracker-creation decision, that's material — present the combined review
-  and ask as usual.
-
-## Why not just widen sign-off's routine-authorization language?
-
-`patterns/approval-sign-off.md` already lets ordinary in-scope work proceed without asking;
-it deliberately keeps that language from reaching into a named sign-off gate. This
-pattern exists because some checkpoints bundle two different things: an irreversible
-or manual step (always ask) and a content-review step whose necessity depends on what
-the content turned out to be (sometimes ask). Splitting the two lets the irreversible
-half stay absolute while the content-review half gets a real, auditable judgment call
-instead of an unconditional stop — which, applied to genuinely trivial content over
-and over, trains developers to rubber-stamp rather than actually review.
